Start a setuid-root server only for the user logged in on the console.

Upstream's Xorg.wrap rule (allowed_users=console): the server takes the
keyboard, the mouse and the display, which belong to whoever is at the
machine.  Without it any account -- one logged in over the network included --
could start the setuid server, grab the input devices and read what is typed
at the console.  The kernel names the console user in /sys/kernel/console_user
(the uid of the console session's login shell).  And, as the wrapper asks,
the server has to be started from the console itself: one of its standard
descriptors is the console.  Being the console user is not enough -- the same
account logged in over the network as well could otherwise start a server on
the machine's display from there.  A server started by root is not elevated
and is not asked.

